  "answer_text": "Here's a breakdown of the typical jersey numbers for **linebackers** in the NFL:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udccb Current NFL Rules (as of 2021 expansion)\n\nLinebackers are currently allowed to wear numbers **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc8 Most Common/Traditional Ranges\n\n| Number Range | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **50\u201359** | The classic, most traditional linebacker numbers |\n| **90\u201399** | Very common for linebackers, especially pass rushers |\n| **40\u201349** | Opened up to linebackers more recently |\n| **1\u201339** | Now allowed under the expanded 2021 rules |\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdc Historical Context\n\n- In **1984**, the NFL allowed linebackers to wear jersey numbers in the **90\u201399** range, since more teams were making use of the 3\u20134 defense and were quickly exhausting numbers for linebackers, who previously were only allowed to wear numbers in the **50\u201359** range.\n\n- The NFL Competition Committee made another change in **2015**, allowing linebackers to use numbers **40\u201349** for the first time.\n\n- In **2021**, NFL owners passed a rule expanding available numbers, which included allowing linebackers to wear **1\u201379** and **90\u201399**. *(Note: The current official range per NFL Football Operations is 1\u201359 and 90\u201399.)*\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf1f Notable Linebacker Numbers\n\n- **56** \u2013 Lawrence Taylor, Derrick Thomas\n- **52** \u2013 Ray Lewis\n- **55** \u2013 Junior Seau, Brian Urlacher\n- **58** \u2013 Jack Lambert, Derrick Brooks\n- **90s** \u2013 Very common for edge rushers and pass-rushing linebackers\n\nSo while **50\u201359** remains the most *classic* and recognizable range for linebackers, you'll frequently see them wearing numbers in the **90s** and increasingly in lower numbers as well.",
